k3s enforces NetworkPolicy — and does not break the two things default-deny usually breaks
============================================================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : LIVE against a real k3s cluster (Multipass VM `k8s-lab`, 17d uptime)
Gate : make check unchanged at 1544 (no production code path changed)

Plan item : NEXT_PLAN "⑥ k3s 검증기 재실행(선택) — flannel은 NetworkPolicy 집행이
            전이되지 않으므로 기판별 재확인 필요"

Outcome   : ENFORCED, side-effects clean, and k3s STILL NOT ADDED to
            PROVEN_ENFORCING_SUBSTRATES. Reason at the bottom — it is not an
            oversight.


Substrate
-----------
  k3s v1.31.4+k3s1, single node (control-plane,master), Ubuntu 24.04
  CNI: flannel (10.42.0.0/16), with k3s's built-in kube-router NetworkPolicy
       controller — the server unit carries no `--disable-network-policy`
  kubeconfig pulled from the VM, server rewritten to 192.168.2.2:6443


1. Does the CNI enforce NetworkPolicy at all?
-----------------------------------------------
  python scripts/verify_netpol_enforcement.py

  first run  : INCONCLUSIVE — "server pod never became Ready". Not treated as a
               failure: the probe's own rule is that a run whose control did not
               work reports nothing. Cause was a cold image cache on the VM
               (`crictl pull` of agnhost:2.47 afterwards returned "up to date",
               i.e. the pull had completed after the 180s Ready wait expired).
  second run : baseline: B -> A reachable (no policy)  ✓   <- control valid
               ENFORCED: default-deny blocked the cross-namespace connection

  The inconclusive-then-green sequence is worth recording rather than tidying
  away: the difference between the two runs was a warm cache, not the cluster,
  and a probe that reported PASS on the first run would have been reporting on
  an experiment whose control never ran.


2. Do the two known side-effects of default-deny bite here?
-------------------------------------------------------------
The kind entry in PROVEN_ENFORCING_SUBSTRATES rests on a live check that kubelet
probes and DNS survive default-deny. kindnet's behaviour does not transfer to
flannel + kube-router, so it was measured rather than assumed. Throwaway
namespace, default-deny applied BEFORE the pod so it is born under the policy:

  kubelet readinessProbe : READY
      Probes originate from the NODE, so no namespaceSelector can ever match
      them. Had kube-router treated them as ordinary ingress, every policied pod
      would go NotReady and it would read as an application regression days
      later. It does not.

  DNS (egress)           : RESOLVED
      k3s-sideeffect-probe.svc.cluster.local,svc.cluster.local,cluster.local
      10.43.0.1   kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local
      The policies set policyTypes: [Ingress] only, so egress should be
      untouched. "Should be" is the kind of claim this repo has been wrong about
      before, so it is measured.

  RESULT: probe=ok dns=ok


Why k3s is still NOT in PROVEN_ENFORCING_SUBSTRATES
-----------------------------------------------------
Adding it is what makes `render_tenancy` emit NetworkPolicies for every env on
that substrate — today that is acme/prod on `k3s-lab`. What the three facts
above establish is that the substrate CAN enforce and that enforcing does not
break workloads. What they do NOT establish is the claim the set actually
licenses: that OUR rendered policy shape isolates correctly — same-tenant
namespaces still reachable, a different tenant denied.

`scripts/verify_tenant_isolation.py` exists for precisely that claim, renders
from the registry rather than inventing its own policy, and asserts the
same/cross axes. It cannot run against k3s-lab: acme/prod is the only env on
that cluster, and the probe needs a SECOND tenant on the SAME cluster to falsify
anything (globex exists for that reason, but only has an env on kind).

Creating globex/prod on k3s-lab would provision real namespaces, quota and
add-ons for another tenant. That is an infrastructure decision for the operator,
not something to invent in order to make a test runnable — so the gate stays
shut and the precondition is recorded in NEXT_PLAN.

Enforcement proven. Semantics unproven. The set means the second one.


Reproduce
-----------
  multipass exec k8s-lab -- sudo cat /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml > k3s.yaml
  sed -i '' 's#https://127.0.0.1:6443#https://192.168.2.2:6443#' k3s.yaml
  KUBECONFIG=k3s.yaml python scripts/verify_netpol_enforcement.py
  KUBECONFIG=k3s.yaml bash scripts/probe_netpol_side_effects.sh


================================================================================
FOLLOW-UP 2026-08-01 — the reason recorded above was true and not the binding one
================================================================================
Everything measured above still holds. What does NOT hold is the section "Why k3s
is still NOT in PROVEN_ENFORCING_SUBSTRATES", which named ONE blocker (no peer
tenant on k3s-lab) and priced it as an infrastructure decision. Measured:

  $ python scripts/verify_tenant_isolation.py --tenant acme --env prod \
        --peer-tenant globex --peer-env prod
  ERROR: acme/prod has 1 namespace(s); the same-tenant leg needs two

  It stops before it ever looks at the peer. acme/prod subscribes one add-on, so
  it renders one namespace, and the same-tenant leg has no second endpoint.

  With that AND the peer removed from the equation (temp registry, measured):
    targets(acme/prod) = ['acme-prod-logging', 'acme-prod-observability'] -> ok
    peers(globex/prod) = ['globex-prod-observability']                    -> ok
    network_policies_apply_to(acme,'prod') = False                        -> exit 2

  THE GATE IS CIRCULAR. The probe that decides whether a substrate belongs in the
  set refuses to run unless the substrate is already in the set. kind passed
  because kind was already inside; the probe has never promoted anything, it has
  regression-tested one member.

  And the cost was priced wrong in the other direction. Live on k8s-lab (20d up):
    kubectl get ns          -> default, kube-node-lease, kube-public, kube-system
    kubectl get netpol -A   -> No resources found
    kubectl get crd | grep capsule|flux -> none (argoproj CRDs only, 26 total)
    non-kube-system pods    -> none
  `acme-prod-*` has never existed. Nothing reconciles this cluster, so a registry
  edit provisions nothing — and adding k3s to the set today would emit policies
  for namespaces that do not exist, protecting zero workloads.

Decision: DECISIONS.md D40 — stays out, for reasons ③ and ④ rather than ②.
Guard: tests/test_substrate_promotion_reachable.py (a membership claim must be
falsifiable with the registry as it stands; adding k3s makes it red).
Investigation: docs/plans/2026-08-01-k3s-proven-substrate.md.
